TASK 5 – Apply Static Members and the Object Life Cycle (3 Marks)
1. Static Variables
Static variables belong to the class rather than individual objects.

In the Parking Management System:
- ticketCounter – Generates unique ticket numbers.
- totalOccupancy – Maintains the total number of occupied slots.
Example:
class ParkingTicket {
    static int ticketCounter = 100;
    int ticketNo;

    ParkingTicket() {
        ticketNo = ++ticketCounter;
    }
}


Why static?

The ticket counter must be shared by all ParkingTicket objects. If it were an instance variable, every object would have a separate counter, which could generate duplicate ticket numbers.
2. Static Methods and Static Block
- Static method: Used to access class-level data without creating an object.
- Static block: Used to initialize static variables when the class is loaded.
class ParkingFacility {
    static int totalOccupancy = 0;

    static {
        totalOccupancy = 0;
    }

    static void displayOccupancy() {
        System.out.println(totalOccupancy);
    }
}


3. Object Life Cycle and Garbage Collection
- A ParkingTicket object is created when a vehicle enters the parking facility.
- Its details are maintained while the vehicle is parked.
- When the vehicle exits, the ticket is closed.
- When no reference points to the ticket object, it becomes eligible for garbage collection.
- Java automatically removes unused objects when garbage collection runs.
4. Passing and Returning Objects
Objects can be passed as method arguments and returned from methods.
ParkingTicket createTicket(String vehicleNo) {
    return new ParkingTicket();
}

void displayTicket(ParkingTicket ticket) {
    System.out.println(ticket.ticketNo);
}


Here, the object reference is passed to displayTicket() and returned by createTicket().
5. Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
If the ticket counter is an instance variable of ParkingFacility, two facilities (North Gate and South Gate) will have separate counters. This may generate duplicate ticket numbers.

Solution:

Use a static counter shared by both facilities to generate globally unique ticket numbers.
Approach	Space for k Facilities
Static counter	O(1) counter storage
Instance counter	O(k) counter storage

For n tickets, ticket objects require O(n) space in both approaches.
